226
00:18:29,860 --> 00:18:33,860
We now return to Axe's Swords and Knives on Modern Marvels.

227
00:18:34,980 --> 00:18:39,540
Swords, more than any other combat weapon, have attained a certain mystical aura,

228
00:18:40,180 --> 00:18:42,540
partly as a result of the difficulty in making them.

229
00:18:42,541 --> 00:18:48,420
It's probably one of the reasons why named swords like Excalibur have entered into our mythology.

230
00:18:48,421 --> 00:18:53,580
A really good sword was a treasure that would be passed on from generation to generation.

231
00:18:54,080 --> 00:19:00,580
At the same time, the sword has a practical dimension to it that one doesn't see in a lot of other weapons.

232
00:19:01,180 --> 00:19:07,020
So the sword takes on a kind of symbolism as a means of defense and protection.

233
00:19:07,021 --> 00:19:14,940
Swords felled unfortunate soldiers as far back as the Mycenaean period during the Trojan War, described by Homer.

234
00:19:15,840 --> 00:19:18,480
The later Romans were also well-equated with swords.

235
00:19:19,680 --> 00:19:26,780
The classic blade weapon of the Roman legionary was called the gladius hispaniensis, the Spanish sword,

236
00:19:27,400 --> 00:19:30,740
and is indeed believed to have originated from Spain.

237
00:19:30,860 --> 00:19:34,600
It's fairly short, mostly a stabbing weapon.

238
00:19:34,601 --> 00:19:40,320
Roman swordsmiths would have provided soldiers with hundreds of thousands of these Spanish swords.

239
00:19:41,440 --> 00:19:45,900
Other cultures besides the Greeks and Romans also incorporated the sword into their militias.

240
00:19:46,620 --> 00:19:50,420
Although the shape of the sword often reflected different methods of combat.

241
00:19:51,420 --> 00:19:55,540
In the Middle East, typically swords tended to be curved.

242
00:19:56,000 --> 00:20:01,640
And one also gets typically curved swords in Japan, for example.

243
00:20:02,540 --> 00:20:07,040
Later swords from the Middle Ages were designed to probe the weaknesses in plate armor.

244
00:20:07,840 --> 00:20:10,560
They were longer and emphasized the thrust over the cut.

245
00:20:11,440 --> 00:20:15,800
Sword fighting required specialized skills tailored to the shape of the sword to be used.

246
00:20:16,580 --> 00:20:20,680
A slender, tapering sword, rigid, would be used to fight heavy armor.

247
00:20:21,240 --> 00:20:26,860
A wide, curved blade would be ideal for cutting at soft cloth armor.

248
00:20:27,760 --> 00:20:32,240
John Clements and his associate Jeff Basham study ancient combat texts

249
00:20:32,241 --> 00:20:36,640
in order to recreate classic sword fighting techniques as part of their organization,

250
00:20:37,280 --> 00:20:40,880
the Association of Renaissance Martial Arts, or ARMA.

251
00:20:41,480 --> 00:20:44,200
In between, you have a range of different weapons,

252
00:20:44,320 --> 00:20:50,800
from very, very slender tapering blades that are ideal for single combat in an urban environment

253
00:20:50,801 --> 00:20:56,240
to different types of cutting and thrusting blades that were suitable for horseback or foot

254
00:20:56,241 --> 00:21:01,040
for facing a variety of armor, from chain ring armor or mail

255
00:21:01,041 --> 00:21:04,020
to different types of heavy leather armor.

256
00:21:05,320 --> 00:21:08,100
Swords designed to thrust, particularly into armor,

257
00:21:08,600 --> 00:21:12,880
have more weight at the hilt or grip, which allows for greater point control.

258
00:21:13,400 --> 00:21:16,920
It can't cut very well because it doesn't have the width or the mass

259
00:21:16,921 --> 00:21:19,620
or the edge bevel to deliver a powerful cut.

260
00:21:20,140 --> 00:21:23,240
So there's a trade-off with each type of sword design,

261
00:21:23,241 --> 00:21:26,760
And no one sword can do everything perfectly well.

262
00:21:28,280 --> 00:21:32,000
Swords designed to cut have more weight toward the point or in the blade.

263
00:21:32,540 --> 00:21:36,520
A wide blade is going to give a much more powerful cut than a narrow blade.

264
00:21:37,040 --> 00:21:40,600
If the blade is thick, however, it's not going to be able to cut very well

265
00:21:40,601 --> 00:21:44,380
because it would have to push too much material aside when it actually penetrates,

266
00:21:44,460 --> 00:21:46,220
when it bites into a target.

267
00:21:53,240 --> 00:22:07,240
Unlike the formalized sport offensive, real sword fighting isn't about scoring points and keeping form.

268
00:22:14,040 --> 00:22:21,000
And it doesn't matter whether you cleave an opponent's body in half or you pierce it through completely.

269
00:22:21,001 --> 00:22:24,620
they're still going to kill the opponent, and that's the purpose of swords.

270
00:22:25,120 --> 00:22:29,180
Swords were designed to kill people, and they did their job very well.

271
00:22:30,540 --> 00:22:35,420
The characteristic hand-and-a-half sword, for example, of the late Middle Ages,

272
00:22:35,660 --> 00:22:39,960
was used in a technique that did not just simply slash with the edge of the sword,

273
00:22:40,140 --> 00:22:42,960
but also thrusted with the point, thrusted with the pommel,

274
00:22:43,280 --> 00:22:44,800
delivered attacks with the quillens.

275
00:22:45,080 --> 00:22:48,000
Indeed, every surface of the sword might be used aggressively,

276
00:22:48,001 --> 00:22:51,160
aggressively, not to mention grappling and wrestling techniques.

277
00:22:51,620 --> 00:22:55,560
You never knew from which part of the sword the next attack was going to come.

278
00:22:56,360 --> 00:23:00,880
This is not the swashbuckling style we're used to seeing in Hollywood costume dramas,

279
00:23:01,120 --> 00:23:05,520
but it's effective in real combat, and it's dead accurate.

280
00:23:09,440 --> 00:23:14,000
Despite their mythic stature, swords were probably never the principal weapon in battle.

281
00:23:14,001 --> 00:23:21,000
That work has been done by weapons that can act at a greater distance.

282
00:23:21,001 --> 00:23:27,200
Probably the greatest burden has been on the spear, because the spear can be at the end

283
00:23:27,201 --> 00:23:31,200
of a shaft that is many feet long.

284
00:23:31,201 --> 00:23:37,500
The sword as a weapon is something that's used up close, and therefore I think would

285
00:23:37,501 --> 00:23:43,500
be that weapon of last resort, used for final dispatch.

286
00:23:43,501 --> 00:23:51,440
Interestingly, an invention gathering momentum during the later Middle Ages would eventually relegate the sword to a largely ceremonial function.

287
00:23:53,120 --> 00:23:59,140
Something better came along, that something was firearms, and the sword fell by the wayside.

288
00:23:59,740 --> 00:24:05,300
Even as sword sank slowly but inevitably into oblivion, there was the odd cross-pollination.

289
00:24:06,160 --> 00:24:08,480
The bayonet was a bladed extension to the gun.

290
00:24:08,481 --> 00:24:14,940
The technique of use of the bayonet and its function on the battlefield is more like the function of a spear.

291
00:24:15,280 --> 00:24:21,300
By this time, pikes essentially have been used and developed as a means of holding off cavalry.

292
00:24:21,880 --> 00:24:27,160
And the bayonet allows the musketeer to become his own pikeman.

293
00:24:28,340 --> 00:24:31,760
American soldiers used short bayonets during World War II

294
00:24:31,761 --> 00:24:36,780
so that the once majestic sword had essentially devolved back into a knife.

295
00:24:36,781 --> 00:24:42,860
With the dominance of the repeating firearm, the sword was almost totally relegated to a ceremonial position.

296
00:24:43,220 --> 00:24:46,300
It became a piece of tacky costume jewelry.

297
00:24:47,020 --> 00:24:50,740
You know, these are certainly past the importance of a sword as a weapon.

298
00:24:51,600 --> 00:24:57,760
Today, there is a small but devoted group of sword collectors who will pay high prices for authentic antique swords.

299
00:24:58,440 --> 00:25:03,100
There is an even smaller group of craftsmen, swordsmiths like Paul Champagne,

300
00:25:03,101 --> 00:25:07,980
who actually make blades to the demanding specifications that ancient swordsmiths followed.

301
00:25:09,220 --> 00:25:11,340
Swords that would be formidable in an ancient battle.

302
00:25:12,580 --> 00:25:16,120
These swords range in price anywhere from $2,000 to $20,000.

303
00:25:17,260 --> 00:25:22,220
The vast majority of manufactured ceremonial swords have no combat value whatsoever.

304
00:25:23,080 --> 00:25:26,220
Their purpose is to hang decoratively on a wall or off a hip.

305
00:25:26,221 --> 00:25:33,720
Today, the most familiar sword is probably the United States Marine Officer's sword,

306
00:25:33,721 --> 00:25:38,620
manufactured by Wilkinson Sword of England, which also produces a number of British military

307
00:25:38,621 --> 00:25:41,660
swords, not to mention razor blades.

308
00:25:41,661 --> 00:25:44,040
It is not designed for combat.

309
00:25:44,041 --> 00:25:53,260
Like swords, axes have also fallen out of favor as combat weapons.

310
00:25:53,261 --> 00:25:58,260
But unlike the sword, the axe has proven to be a highly useful all-purpose blade.

311
00:25:58,261 --> 00:26:03,260
Next, the brutal power of the axe.

312
00:26:03,261 --> 00:26:10,260
England's Queen Elizabeth employed four ceremonial swords during her coronation in 1953.

313
00:26:10,261 --> 00:26:17,260
The sword of mercy, the sword of spiritual justice, the sword of temporal justice, and the sword of state.

314
00:26:17,261 --> 00:26:21,260
Axe's swords and knives will return on Modern Marvels.

315
00:26:23,260 --> 00:26:26,760
We now return to Axes, Swords, and Knives on Modern Marvels.

316
00:26:29,500 --> 00:26:31,840
Klondike days in Eagle River, Wisconsin.

317
00:26:32,460 --> 00:26:34,560
Temperature, 28 degrees Fahrenheit.

318
00:26:35,340 --> 00:26:36,900
Forecast, flurries.

319
00:26:37,680 --> 00:26:44,240
Today's attractions include dog sledding, chainsaw carving, a horse pole, and pony rides.

320
00:26:44,241 --> 00:26:47,540
But the biggest crowds are gathering for the lumberjack competition.

321
00:26:47,541 --> 00:26:54,540
scheduled events include two-man bucking, underhand shot, pole climbing, and the springboard.

322
00:26:58,540 --> 00:27:01,540
And always a crowd pleaser is the axe throw.

323
00:27:05,540 --> 00:27:07,540
While you're 20 feet from the target,

324
00:27:11,540 --> 00:27:14,540
the target is 5 feet high from the bullseye,

325
00:27:14,541 --> 00:27:21,540
and you just got to get the right rotation on the axle where the axe sticks.

326
00:27:25,540 --> 00:27:29,005
The axe is anywhere between two and three pounds.

327
00:27:29,006 --> 00:27:32,540
I use an axe that's specially made in New Zealand.

328
00:27:32,541 --> 00:27:36,148
Some of the competitors use that particular brand.

329
00:27:36,149 --> 00:27:39,540
All the axes we use are all double-bitted axes.

330
00:27:39,541 --> 00:27:46,320
Specially made for axe throwing in competitions.

331
00:27:49,900 --> 00:27:57,220
These events will draw close to 12,000 spectators over two days, with prize money totaling $7,500.

332
00:27:59,180 --> 00:28:03,700
Many competitors use specially made axes that would be impractical for timber cutting.

333
00:28:04,780 --> 00:28:08,860
For one thing, they are double-edged. Good for throwing, but dangerous for chopping.

334
00:28:09,540 --> 00:28:15,420
For another, they're too small, and at around three pounds, too light to be much use for a serious lumberjack.

335
00:28:15,620 --> 00:28:22,500
This ax is made in Sweden, and they're about $95 in this country, and that's all I use it for.

336
00:28:22,580 --> 00:28:23,640
I don't use it for anything else.

337
00:28:24,080 --> 00:28:28,480
You don't really need to have any muscles to ax throw.

338
00:28:28,580 --> 00:28:30,260
Ax throwing is a lot like shooting a free throw.

339
00:28:30,760 --> 00:28:34,140
You know, some people can shoot free throws well, some people can't, you know.

340
00:28:34,520 --> 00:28:36,140
And it's the same basic thing.

341
00:28:36,141 --> 00:28:42,560
Yeah, except that if you miss a free throw, nobody's in danger of having a gaping basketball wound in his or her head.

342
00:28:44,340 --> 00:28:50,140
Today's axe-throwing competitions feature an exceptionally frivolous exercise for an utterly practical tool.

343
00:28:51,660 --> 00:28:55,720
And axes are simple tools designed to chop without much finesse.

344
00:28:57,040 --> 00:29:02,280
Unlike a knife which can carefully slice or fillet, an axe is like a sharp club.

345
00:29:02,900 --> 00:29:06,700
It requires a big, forceful swing in order to launch its heavy head.

346
00:29:07,800 --> 00:29:10,380
The first axes were made of stone,

347
00:29:10,900 --> 00:29:13,940
and these axes were used by the farmers to clear the land.

348
00:29:14,780 --> 00:29:18,660
As they would chip and be broken down, they would become smaller.

349
00:29:18,661 --> 00:29:25,480
And so this example has been reground several times and has ended up quite short.

350
00:29:26,200 --> 00:29:28,960
With the advent of metallurgy during the Bronze Age,

351
00:29:29,340 --> 00:29:31,660
much stronger axes could be forged in kilns.

352
00:29:32,280 --> 00:29:36,300
The axe was not only a notably useful tool, it was also a formidable weapon.

353
00:29:36,960 --> 00:29:40,000
What it lacked in subtlety, it made up for in raw power.

354
00:29:40,600 --> 00:29:46,240
The characteristic feature of an axe is that the weight is entirely concentrated in the head.

355
00:29:46,241 --> 00:29:50,060
Now this means the axe delivers a tremendously powerful blow.

356
00:29:50,540 --> 00:29:55,280
The disadvantage of an axe, of course, is that because the weight is all in the head, it's much slower.

357
00:29:55,281 --> 00:30:02,540
What this means in short terms is that the axe is used chiefly in a pretty aggressive style.

358
00:30:02,960 --> 00:30:07,860
Some Bronze Age combat axes tried to mitigate the weapon's slow delivery with a double edge,

359
00:30:08,380 --> 00:30:11,760
so that a return swing could be just as deadly as an initial swing.

360
00:30:12,660 --> 00:30:16,320
The axe actually has some substantial advantages over the sword.

361
00:30:16,920 --> 00:30:20,720
It's technologically easier to produce. It means the axe is cheap.

362
00:30:20,721 --> 00:30:26,680
And so the axe is an excellent weapon to use if you are just an ordinary foot soldier

363
00:30:26,681 --> 00:30:29,960
who doesn't have an awful lot of money at his disposal.

364
00:30:30,620 --> 00:30:33,920
An axe is capable of delivering a more powerful blow than a sword.

365
00:30:34,560 --> 00:30:39,040
This became strategically important in the latter Middle Ages after plate armor was introduced.

366
00:30:40,040 --> 00:30:44,540
Basically, an ordinary sword is not going to be able to chop through plate armor.

367
00:30:44,620 --> 00:30:46,640
You've got to find some kind of an alternative.

368
00:30:47,380 --> 00:30:51,380
The battle axe came off the field of combat to become a weapon of justice,

369
00:30:51,660 --> 00:30:53,740
as the logical tool for beheadings.

370
00:30:55,280 --> 00:30:58,320
Though beheadings were familiar to cultures all over the world,

371
00:30:58,460 --> 00:31:00,440
they are particularly associated with England,

372
00:31:01,100 --> 00:31:04,480
thanks in part to Henry VIII and Anne Boleyn.

373
00:31:05,760 --> 00:31:08,680
These big execution axes were often curved,

374
00:31:08,980 --> 00:31:10,720
in order to make contact at a point,

375
00:31:11,300 --> 00:31:14,220
and thus more easily penetrate the flesh and bone.

376
00:31:16,640 --> 00:31:22,400
Axe manufacturer was similar, though far less complicated than sword manufacturer,

377
00:31:22,760 --> 00:31:25,960
and began with a forged brick, or billet, of metal.

378
00:31:26,340 --> 00:31:30,280
You start with a billet, which is common to swords and knives and axes.

379
00:31:30,800 --> 00:31:33,640
You have to start making the hole for the handle to go through.

380
00:31:34,500 --> 00:31:39,000
So you heat it up, again yellow hot, and a simple tool, a simple punch,

381
00:31:39,480 --> 00:31:41,180
and drive it through to make a hole.

382
00:31:42,520 --> 00:31:46,220
Now that hole ends up being long and narrow, obviously too narrow for a handle.

383
00:31:46,640 --> 00:31:53,520
and use a couple simple tools, very simple, to open it up, again driven through by hand,

384
00:31:54,600 --> 00:31:57,960
and then take another tool through the center to give it some of the roundness.

385
00:31:58,500 --> 00:32:03,940
Then all you have to do is take this part on the hammer or with strikers and draw it out and shape it to an edge.

386
00:32:04,420 --> 00:32:06,860
Flatten out the rest of it and you have a primitive axe.

387
00:32:08,620 --> 00:32:15,020
Even as axe production entered the Industrial Revolution, the simple methods used to make an axe changed little.

388
00:32:15,021 --> 00:32:22,020
Hand craftsmanship continued, as this early 20th century film taken at a Fiskars brand

389
00:32:22,021 --> 00:32:25,020
manufacturing plant in Finland shows.

390
00:32:25,021 --> 00:32:31,020
These days things have changed.

391
00:32:31,021 --> 00:32:36,140
Unlike traditional hickory wood handles, those of fiberglass are hard to break and won't

392
00:32:36,141 --> 00:32:41,820
shrink or swell in changing temperatures and humidity.

393
00:32:41,821 --> 00:32:46,480
The center of gravity is located in the axe head, creating a more ergonomic axe.

394
00:32:48,100 --> 00:32:51,260
Fiskars tests each of its axes for strength and durability.

395
00:32:54,340 --> 00:32:59,100
Fiskars even makes axe accessories, like sharpeners for its many specialized axes.

396
00:33:02,480 --> 00:33:08,160
These Fiskars axes will be shipped all over the world and used to fell trees, not opponents.

397
00:33:08,161 --> 00:33:15,060
As versatile a tool as the axe is, modern warriors need something subtler, more sophisticated.

398
00:33:16,060 --> 00:33:20,540
Something lethal enough to fillet an opponent, yet delicate enough to spread butter.

399
00:33:22,000 --> 00:33:25,720
Next, the evolution and specialization of the knife.

400
00:33:27,800 --> 00:33:31,860
We now return to axes, swords, and knives on Modern Marvels.

401
00:33:33,080 --> 00:33:35,900
Weapons like the machine gun may have killed the sword.

402
00:33:36,720 --> 00:33:39,700
But they didn't entirely eliminate blades for the soldier.

403
00:33:40,560 --> 00:33:42,940
What remained was the stealthy knife.

404
00:33:46,420 --> 00:33:50,540
One of the great advantages the knife has over the sword is that it can be concealed.

405
00:33:52,060 --> 00:33:55,240
Knives come in a variety of sizes and shapes, lengths.

406
00:33:55,740 --> 00:33:57,480
Some are single-edged, some are double-edged.

407
00:33:57,880 --> 00:34:03,240
Daggers are primarily used for killing, and daggers have two edges, narrow to a point.

408
00:34:03,241 --> 00:34:07,640
once nearly everyone men and women carried knives

409
00:34:07,641 --> 00:34:12,740
exquisite examples survive dating back through the late stone age

410
00:34:12,741 --> 00:34:18,940
much like swords the shape of the knife determines how it will be used in combat

411
00:34:18,941 --> 00:34:26,600
and this hinges on whether the knife is a cutter or a thruster

412
00:34:26,601 --> 00:34:32,000
the slashing knives tend to be single-edged whereas the thrusting knives tend to be more

413
00:34:32,001 --> 00:34:36,960
double-edge. Ron Harris is an edged weapons expert who, with Paul Beric,

414
00:34:36,961 --> 00:34:42,820
demonstrates some basic knife fighting techniques. The problem with the

415
00:34:42,821 --> 00:34:48,380
lethality is a debatable issue. When you slash someone, it might take them three

416
00:34:48,381 --> 00:34:52,900
to five minutes to bleed out. When you thrust someone, you disrupt the vital

417
00:34:52,901 --> 00:34:57,500
functions, the organs, and so on. They might stop instantly. They'll bleed inside.

418
00:34:57,501 --> 00:35:01,680
The problem with slashing is that you might be in a fight for a long time.

419
00:35:01,681 --> 00:35:05,680
three to five minutes might mean that you both end up in death grips.

420
00:35:07,860 --> 00:35:11,920
Knives were too valuable as a multi-purpose tool to go the way of the sword.

421
00:35:13,040 --> 00:35:16,240
Firearms merely removed them from the realm of principal combat weapon.

422
00:35:17,180 --> 00:35:21,160
In fact, the utility knife is still a vital component of any soldier's battle gear.

423
00:35:22,860 --> 00:35:25,460
Not to mention weekend warriors of all stripes.

424
00:35:25,461 --> 00:35:32,460
Nowhere is that made more apparent than in the phenomenally successful Swiss Army knives.

425
00:35:32,461 --> 00:35:35,460
I have two knives always in my pocket.

426
00:35:35,461 --> 00:35:38,460
One of them is the Midnight Manager.

427
00:35:38,461 --> 00:35:41,460
It has a small letter opener.

428
00:35:41,461 --> 00:35:48,460
It has a small, very practical ballpoint pen and an LED light.

429
00:35:48,461 --> 00:35:53,460
The next knife I always carry in my pocket is the Voyager

430
00:35:53,461 --> 00:36:03,960
because this has a larger blade or harder work and it also tells me the time and wakes me up in the morning.

431
00:36:05,600 --> 00:36:08,840
Charles Elsner ought to have a couple of Swiss Army knives with him.

432
00:36:09,180 --> 00:36:12,700
He's president of Victorinox, the company that makes Swiss Army knives.

433
00:36:13,280 --> 00:36:19,380
In fact, his great-grandfather Karl Elsner founded the company over 100 years ago in Ebach, Switzerland.

434
00:36:19,381 --> 00:36:31,820
In 1890, the Swiss Army had decided to buy soldier knives for all the soldiers of the Swiss Army.

435
00:36:33,040 --> 00:36:42,780
And in the beginning, there was a company in Solingen, Germany, who started to supply these knives.

436
00:36:43,900 --> 00:36:47,220
Elsner thought those knives ought to be supplied by Swiss bladesmiths.

437
00:36:47,221 --> 00:36:52,220
So he came up with a plan to wrest the knife commission from the Germans.

438
00:36:52,221 --> 00:36:58,220
Charles Elzner himself, he was a little bit too small for the order,

439
00:36:58,221 --> 00:37:05,220
and therefore he decided to found the Swiss Knife Association

440
00:37:05,221 --> 00:37:10,220
with the goal to supply the Swiss Army with the soldier knives.

441
00:37:12,220 --> 00:37:15,220
Within a year, all the other association members had quit,

442
00:37:15,221 --> 00:37:17,220
and Elsnir nearly went bankrupt.

443
00:37:19,220 --> 00:37:22,220
Although he was unable to produce the volume of knives the Germans could,

444
00:37:23,220 --> 00:37:27,220
his knives were better made and contained extras that endeared them to the soldiers.

445
00:37:28,220 --> 00:37:35,220
In 1897, he developed a newer and a lighter model for officers.

446
